---
import CodeBlock from '../CodeBlock.astro';
import StepCard from '../StepCard.astro';
---

<div class="sec" id="s3">
  <div class="num">03</div>
  <h2>Install Claude Code</h2>
  <p class="lede">
    The terminal looks unfriendly. You only need a few commands.
  </p>
  <p>
    A terminal is a window for typing commands. On a Mac, open Terminal using
    Spotlight. On Windows, open PowerShell from Start. The steps here cover
    those two systems; Linux users should follow the linked installation page.
    Press Enter after typing or pasting each terminal command.
  </p>
  <p>
    Claude Code requires internet access and an eligible Claude account or
    supported API access. Check the current account requirements on
    <a href="https://code.claude.com/docs/en/setup#authenticate"
      >Claude Code's setup page</a
    > before paying. For this exercise, I would use an eligible subscription rather
    than set up separately billed API access.
  </p>
  <div class="steps">
    <StepCard number="1" title={'Install and check it'}>
      <p>
        On the setup page, check that your computer meets the requirements. Copy
        the install command for macOS or Windows PowerShell into your terminal
        and press Enter. This downloads and runs Anthropic's installer, so take
        it from that official page, not an AI reply.
      </p>
      <p>
        When it finishes, open a new terminal window and run the check below. A
        version number means the installation worked. If it says the command was
        not found, stop here and use the installation troubleshooting link on
        the setup page.
      </p>
    </StepCard>
  </div>
  <CodeBlock
    label={'Terminal: check installation'}
    code={'claude --version'}
    copy
  />
  <div class="steps">
    <StepCard number="2" title={'Make and enter your folder'}>
      <p>
        In Finder on a Mac or File Explorer on Windows, make a new folder called
        <code class="i">my-site</code>. In the terminal, type <code class="i"
          >cd</code
        >
        followed by a space. On a Mac, drag the folder into the window. On Windows,
        copy its full location from File Explorer's address bar and paste it between
        straight double quotes. Press Enter. This changes which folder the terminal
        is working in.
      </p>
    </StepCard>
    <StepCard number="3" title={'Start and sign in'}>
      <p>
        Run the command below to request auto mode, which reduces routine
        permission prompts. Follow the browser sign-in prompts with your
        eligible Claude account. If asked to trust a folder, check it is your
        new <code class="i">my-site</code> folder.
      </p>
    </StepCard>
  </div>
  <CodeBlock
    label={'Terminal: start with auto mode'}
    code={'claude --permission-mode auto'}
    copy
  />
  <p>
    Auto mode uses a separate AI safety checker for some actions; routine reads
    and edits inside the project can run without it. It is not bypass
    permissions, a sandbox or a guarantee of safety. For this practice site, you
    approve the overall plan and check the finished page rather than answering a
    question for every edit.
  </p>
  <p>
    Check the active mode shown in the terminal. Availability depends on your
    version, model and account settings. If auto mode is unavailable, Claude
    Code may fall back to prompting you. If the flag is rejected, start with
    <code class="i">claude --permission-mode default</code> instead. That setting
    normally asks before edits and commands, subject to permission rules; its screen
    label varies by version. A permission prompt shows a proposed action and choices
    to allow or deny it. Read the action, use the displayed keys to select your choice,
    then press Enter where instructed. If unclear, stop and ask for help rather than
    approve everything. See the current
    <a href="https://code.claude.com/docs/en/permission-modes"
      >permission docs</a
    >.
  </p>
  <h3>Give it standing instructions</h3>
  <p>
    <code class="i">CLAUDE.md</code> is a plain text file of project instructions
    Claude Code reads at the start of a session, so you do not have to repeat your
    preferences every time. <strong
      >It is guidance, not enforced protection.</strong
    > The rule about photos below does not lock the folder or prevent Claude from
    modifying it. That is why you use copies and keep backups outside the project.
  </p>
  <p>
    In Claude Code, type "Show me a plan to create CLAUDE.md with exactly the
    following text, and change nothing else. Wait for my ok", then paste this
    block into the same message. Read the plan, then give your ok.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock
    label={'Example CLAUDE.md'}
    code={'# About this project\n\nThis is my one-page personal website.\n\n# Rules\n\nShow me a short plan and wait for my ok before changing files.\nExplain things briefly in plain English. I am not a programmer.\nDo not add anything I did not ask for.\nDo not modify the photos folder or work outside this project.\nDo not upload or publish files, install software or change permission settings without asking me.'}
    copy
  />
  <p>
    Ask it to show the saved file so you can check it. The request to wait is
    still only an instruction. For a stronger planning checkpoint, prefix a task
    with <code class="i">/plan</code>: Claude Code's plan mode blocks source
    edits until you approve the plan, provided bypass permissions are not
    enabled. You can then choose auto mode to carry out the approved plan.
  </p>
</div>
